# Tunnel-auth secret — EXAMPLE FILE. Contains no secret; safe to commit.
#
# The backend enforces its own gate on every request except /health, /docs,
# /openapi.json and /redoc. If it cannot resolve a secret it REJECTS everything
# (fail-closed), which shows up in the browser as an endless WebSocket reconnect
# storm -- the handshake never completes, so the client cannot see the 403 and
# reports a generic "WebSocket error occurred" instead.
#
# There is NO .env loader in this repo (no python-dotenv in
# backend/requirements.txt). `_env_token()` calls os.getenv() directly, so the
# value must be in the environment of the process that serves requests. Writing
# it into some .env file next to the code does nothing.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SETUP — run once
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# USE ABSOLUTE PATHS. The backend is started from either the repo root or
# backend/, so a $PWD-relative path is valid in one and broken in the other.
# That mistake was made and cost a cycle (2026-09-30): the shell redirect failed
# with "No such file or directory" and nothing downstream said why.
#
#   R1=/kaggle/working/R1v0.1          # adjust if your checkout is elsewhere
#   mkdir -p $R1/backend/keys
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))" \
#       > $R1/backend/keys/tunnel_auth.token
#   chmod 600 $R1/backend/keys/tunnel_auth.token
#   export TUNNEL_AUTH_TOKEN_FILE=$R1/backend/keys/tunnel_auth.token
#
# Confirm the file exists BEFORE exporting -- a failed `>` leaves no file and
# the export then points at nothing:
#
#   ls -l $TUNNEL_AUTH_TOKEN_FILE && wc -c < $TUNNEL_AUTH_TOKEN_FILE
#
# Only the PATH goes in the env var. That is the point of the file form: the
# secret is written once and never re-pasted, so a cell re-run, a restart, or a
# fresh kernel cannot lose it. backend/keys/ and *.token are gitignored.
#
# If you start the backend from backend/ instead, the env var must STILL be the
# absolute path above. Only the export, not the file's location, is what the
# loader reads.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FRONTEND MUST HAVE THE SAME VALUE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The browser cannot set headers on a WebSocket handshake, so the secret travels
# as `?tunnel_token=` on both REST and WS. It must be in hosting/.env.local as:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_TUNNEL_AUTH_TOKEN=<the contents of the token file>
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* is inlined at build time — restart `npm run dev` after changing
# it, or the running dev server keeps using the old value.
#
# The two sides are compared with hmac.compare_digest, which reports no detail.
# A trailing newline or a truncated paste produces a 403 storm identical to a
# missing secret. The loader strips whitespace, but a partial paste will not be
# caught for you. Compare explicitly when debugging:
#
#   diff <(cat backend/keys/tunnel_auth.token) \
#        <(grep NEXT_PUBLIC_TUNNEL_AUTH_TOKEN hosting/.env.local | cut -d= -f2-)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DISABLING THE GATE (local dev only — never on a public box)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   export TUNNEL_AUTH_REQUIRED=0
#
# Precedence when both are set: TUNNEL_AUTH_TOKEN inline wins, then
# TUNNEL_AUTH_TOKEN_FILE.